In Larry's firmware, Base, Medium and Full modes will pad zeros if 10b, 12b, or 14b. No zero padding if 8b.
DECA mode never pads zero.  Also, 10bit data is packed and need to be unpacked as explained below:

--Packing 10bit Pixel Data--
MIPI RAW10 packing refers to how 10-bit pixel data from an image sensor is organized into a stream of bytes for transmission over a MIPI CSI-2 interface. Four 10-bit pixels are packed into five bytes, with the higher 8 bits of each pixel transmitted first, followed by the lower 2 bits. This packing method ensures efficient use of the data bandwidth while maintaining the 10-bit resolution. 

Here's a breakdown of the packing process: 

First byte: Contains bits [9:2] of the first pixel (P1).
Second byte: Contains bits [9:2] of the second pixel (P2).
Third byte: Contains bits [9:2] of the third pixel (P3).
Fourth byte: Contains bits [9:2] of the fourth pixel (P4).
Fifth byte: Contains bits [1:0] of P1, bits [1:0] of P2, bits [1:0] of P3, and bits [1:0] of P4, effectively packing the least significant bits of all four pixels.

This method allows for a continuous stream of pixel data, even though the underlying data is 10-bit, while minimizing the number of bytes needed for transmission. If the image width is not a multiple of four pixels, padding with zeros may be necessary to complete the last byte. 

